            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Quadrilaterals"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.852,
            0.071,
            0.881,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "59"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.11,
            0.883,
            0.187
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Therefore "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle A + \\angle B = \\angle B + \\angle C = 180^{\\circ}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . As in the proof of (b), we use the fact that AB is a transversal for the pair BC and AD. By "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle \\mathrm{A} + \\angle \\mathrm{B} = 180^{\\circ}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " we deduce BC ‖AD. Similarly, we get AB ‖DC by using "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle B + \\angle C = 180^{\\circ}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.135,
            0.21,
            0.4,
            0.233
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Think and Reflect"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.247,
            0.854,
            0.344
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The angles of a quadrilateral add up to "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "3 6 0 ^ { \\circ }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Therefore, if the opposite angles are equal, what can we say about adjacent angles? Is the converse of your answer true? Conclude that Theorem 3 can also be stated as follows. “If each pair of adjacent angles in a quadrilateral ABCD…then ABCD is a parallelogram.” Fill in the blank."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.365,
            0.881,
            0.405
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Theorem 4 (converse of (c)):",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " A quadrilateral whose diagonals bisect each other is a parallelogram."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.409,
            0.882,
            0.489
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Proof:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Let ABCD be this quadrilateral. Let E be the intersection point of the diagonals AC and BD. As "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm{EA} = \\mathrm{EC}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "EB = \\pm  DD"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , we have "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\Delta  AED \\cong \\Delta  CEB"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " by SAS. Now using "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle DAE = \\angle ECB"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " along with transversal AC, we get AD ‖BC. Similarly, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\Delta  EAB  \\cong \\Delta  ECD"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " gives AB ‖ DC."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.423,
            0.492,
            0.617,
            0.601
