              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 12.9: Converse to (c)"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.634,
            0.881,
            0.675
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Finally, there is one more useful test to check if a given quadrilateral ABCD is a parallelogram."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.679,
            0.881,
            0.72
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Theorem 5 (equal parallel sides test)",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ": A quadrilateral with one pair of equal and parallel opposite sides is a parallelogram."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.14,
            0.745,
            0.41,
            0.863
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 11,
              "bbox": [
                0.14,
                0.745,
                0.41,
                0.863
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_8_image_11.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 12,
              "bbox": [
                0.184,
                0.873,
                0.28,
                0.893
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 12.10"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.456,
            0.735,
            0.881,
            0.911
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Proof:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Suppose in a quadrilateral ABCD we have AB ‖DC and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm{AB} = \\mathrm{DC}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Let E be the intersection point of the diagonals AC and BD. (Why must the diagonals intersect? See Exercise Set 12.1, Q3.) We have "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\Delta  EAB  \\cong \\Delta  ECD"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " by ASA. So the diagonals bisect each other. By the converse of (c), ABCD is a parallelogram."
            }
          ]
        }
